      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The contemporary landscape of Social Capital Theory is incredibly multifaceted, extending far beyond early formulations from **Coleman** and **Portes**. Initial groundwork, rooted in **Bourdieu**'s conceptualizations of resources, was expanded by **Putnam**’s focus on civic engagement, yet the structural perspective offered by **Granovetter** regarding tie strength remains crucial. Furthering these ideas, **Robert D. Putnam**'s diverse body of work has been dialogued with more granular studies by **Lin** and **Nan Lin** on network structure. To account for more complex institutional settings, researchers such as **Fukuyama**, **Alejandro Portes**, and **Mark Granovetter** continue to refine frameworks. Specifically, **Ronald Burt**'s centrality measures are often contrasted with analyses from **Robert Mayer** and **Stephen J. Gould** concerning relational dynamics, while **James Coleman**’s contributions meet modern interpretations from **Alejandro Portes** and **Francis Fukuyama**. More niche but vital contributions come from **Leigh van der Bulck**, **Misako Chiba**, **Nan Lin**, and **Sandstrom**, who delve into specific contexts. Finally, the breadth is captured by incorporating insights from **Pierre",
      "source": "target",
